{"compressedText":"# Field Guide: Preparing a Home for a 72-Hour Power Outage\n\nThis guide prepares an ordinary household to manage safely and comfortably through a power outage lasting up to seventy-two hours. It assumes a temperate climate and no backup generator. Work through the sections in order; the earlier ones cover the preparations that matter most.\n\n## Water\n\nStore four litres of water per person per day—twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours. Half is for drinking, half for cooking and basic hygiene. Use food-grade containers, keep them out of direct sunlight, and replace stored water every six months. If a storm is forecast and you have warning, also fill the bathtub; bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never for drinking.\n\n## Food\n\nKeep a dedicated shelf of non-perishable food for three days. Good choices: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, and shelf-stable milk. Choose foods needing no cooking, and own a manual can opener, since an electric one is useless. Eat the refrigerator's perishable food first, then the freezer's, and only then open the emergency shelf.\n\n## Refrigerator and freezer\n\nAn unopened refrigerator keeps food safe for about four hours. A full freezer holds a safe temperature for about forty-eight hours, but a half-full one for only about twenty-four, so keep the freezer consolidated and full—even if containers of frozen water fill the space. Keep both doors closed as much as possible and tape a reminder note to each. Discard any perishable food left more than two hours above four degrees Celsius; when in doubt, throw it out.\n\n## Light\n\nUse battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as your primary light, and avoid candles entirely, since they are a leading cause of house fires during outages. Store at least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light. Keep one headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, placed where they can be found in the dark.\n\n## Heat and safety\n\nNever run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window, because each produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal. Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep. In cold weather, choose one small room on a south-facing side, close its door, and gather everyone there; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater. If the indoor temperature falls below ten degrees Celsius and you cannot stay warm, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.\n\n## Communication and information\n\nKeep a battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts, and learn your local emergency station's frequency before the outage. Charge every phone and power bank whenever a storm is forecast; a full modern twenty-thousand milliamp-hour power bank recharges a typical phone roughly four times. Put your phone in low-power mode at the outage's start, and agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every household member will text, since texts get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.\n\n## Medical needs\n\nKeep a first-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, rotating them so they stay in date. Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally stays usable at room temperature for up to twenty-eight days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist beforehand. Write your pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control numbers on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book.\n\n## When the power returns\n\nWait five minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise. Check the freezer: if food still contains ice crystals, it can be safely refrozen. Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries. Finally, write a one-page note on what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one gap before the next outage.","expectedMetric":627,"hardConstraintNote":"Still a self-contained instruction document: no external references introduced, no cross-links, no reliance on removed context. All eight original sections (Water, Food, Refrigerator and freezer, Light, Heat and safety, Communication and information, Medical needs, When the power returns) remain in the same order under the same headers, the intro still states scope (72 h, temperate climate, no generator, work in order), and every actionable instruction is complete and unambiguous on its own — a reader with only this text can execute every step. expectedMetric = 627 words by the frontier rule (whitespace-split tokens of the full markdown, including the '#'/'##' header markers), a hand count summed section by section; well below the 730 baseline (GR-1) and within the line-editor lever's ~610-word rough ceiling band.","killedLeverCitations":["KILLED_LEVERS.md is empty (only the placeholder row 'none yet'); no K-id fences any lever, so this proposal re-treads nothing (GR-6)."],"lens":"line-editor","leverId":"line-editor-tighten-pass","rationale":"A one-seat line edit: I cut throat-clearing (\"This guide explains how to...\", \"It is written for...\"), collapsed redundant restatements (\"which means twelve litres\" → em-dash apposition; \"an electric one will be useless\" → \"since an electric one is useless\"), converted passive/nominal phrasing to active (\"know the frequency of your local emergency station\" → \"learn your local emergency station's frequency\"; \"Any perishable food that has spent...should be discarded\" → \"Discard any perishable food left...\"), and merged filler connectives — without deleting a single quantity, duration, temperature, item, or ordered step. All eight section headers and their order are unchanged, so gate and hard-constraint risk stay minimal. Every fact from the original survives: 4 L/person/day and 12 L/72 h with the half-drinking/half-hygiene split; food-grade containers, no sunlight, 6-month replacement; bathtub caveat (flush/wash, never drink); 3-day shelf with the full six-item list; no-cook + manual can opener; fridge-then-freezer-then-shelf order; 4 h fridge, 48 h full / 24 h half freezer, frozen-water filler, both doors closed + note on each, discard >2 h above 4 C; LED lanterns/headlamps, no candles (house-fire cause), one light/person + two spare battery sets each, headlamp per bed + kitchen lantern findable in dark; the indoors/garage/window CO ban, CO alarm per sleeping floor, south-facing small room + layers/blankets over heaters, relocate below 10 C; hand-crank radio + known frequency, charge on forecast, 20,000 mAh ≈ 4 recharges, low-power mode, one out-of-area text contact; first-aid + 7-day meds rotated, insulin ~28 days room temp with pharmacist confirmation, pharmacy/doctor/poison-control on paper; 5-minute wait, ice-crystal refreeze rule, restock within one week starting water+batteries, one-page after-note fixing at least one gap.","title":"Single sentence-level tightening pass; every fact and section order preserved"}